RISC-V zkVM: give small integer locals and INT spill temps a whole aligned 8-byte stack home (applies after
perf-74 and perf-75).

ZisK charges a 1-, 2- or 4-byte access, or one not aligned to 8 bytes, several times an aligned 8-byte one: it
runs through the MemAlign state machine (a 4-byte store reads and rewrites the word). The JIT gives an INT (or
smaller) local a 4-byte home and spills a register to a 4-byte temp, so every store to it is an sw and every
reload an lw. On the Nethermind guest these frame accesses were about a fifth of all MemAlign rows; most were a
handful of trie and RLP methods keeping a span length or a bool parameter on the frame across calls.

Under JitZkBumpAddr (every zkVM libc, never a real riscv64 target) such a home is now a whole aligned 8-byte
slot (lvaIsZkWideHome: an integer local of at most 4 bytes that is not address-exposed and not a field of a
dependently promoted struct, including a parameter passed in one register and a local a parameter register
maps to), and an INT spill temp is 8 bytes and 8-byte aligned. The emitter widens the frame accesses at offset 0
of such a slot (lvaIsZkWideHomeAccess):
- a store that covers the local (not a partial LCL_FLD store) becomes sd, so the slot always holds the whole
  register that was saved;
- a load becomes ld when the local is a register candidate or a spill temp. ld restores the register bit for bit,
  where lw/lh/lb would re-extend its low bits. Code is correct for the register value it had before the spill,
  and nothing relies on a reload extending it, except two cases that are kept narrow or fixed up:
  - a local that is not a register candidate is read at every use and Lowering::IsInt32SignExtendedInReg trusts
    those loads to be extended (it may also have LCL_FLD stores): its loads stay narrow;
  - a small parameter normalized once on entry (perf-75) has its home filled with the register as the caller
    passed it: its loads stay narrow.
  A local Lowering took to be sign-extended (lvSignExtendedInt) must hold an extended value in its slot as it
  does in its register: genCodeForStoreLclVar sign-extends a call result stored straight to the slot, as it
  already did for a store to the register, and genSpillOrAddRegisterParam sign-extends a mapped parameter
  register in place before homing it (perf-74 did that only for one homed in a register).

Little-endian keeps the low bytes of the slot equal to the narrow value, so a narrow load that stays (lwu, a
partial LCL_FLD, a non-candidate) reads the same bytes as before.

Nethermind stateless guest on ZisK (ziskemu 1.3.2 -X --mem-full-stats, plan from cargo-zisk execute), block
26055159 / 26112793: cost -0.80% / -0.75%; MemAlign rows -23.0% / -18.8%; steps +6,069 / +11,993 (+0.01%, the
sign extensions above and lui/add for frame offsets that no longer fit 12 bits). SP1 cycles +287 / +454.
Outputs identical on 11 mainnet blocks.

